Give each time namespace a realtime offset that can change at any time

Harmony's fault search moves one node's wall clock while every node runs in
one guest. Time namespaces already offset CLOCK_MONOTONIC and CLOCK_BOOTTIME,
but those offsets are fixed once a task joins, and nothing offsets
CLOCK_REALTIME, which Go's time.Now, gettimeofday and time read.

Add a realtime offset, in nanoseconds, to struct timens_offsets.
/proc/PID/timens_offsets accepts a "realtime" line before and after tasks
join, and shows it. The monotonic and boottime offsets keep their freeze.
CLOCK_REALTIME, CLOCK_REALTIME_COARSE and CLOCK_TAI reads, gettimeofday and
time add the offset. Absolute CLOCK_REALTIME, CLOCK_REALTIME_ALARM and
CLOCK_TAI deadlines in clock_nanosleep, timer_settime, timerfd and futex
waits convert to host time when they are armed. A deadline armed before an
offset change keeps its host expiry.

The vDSO serves the offset through the namespace VVAR page that already
carries the monotonic and boottime offsets. A runtime change bumps the system
VVAR sequence count around the page update, and the namespace readers load
the offsets inside their sequence loops, so a reader never combines the
seconds of one offset with the nanoseconds of another. The time vDSO entry
adds the offset's seconds.

The kernel's own realtime uses, such as file timestamps, stay on host time.

This patch is Linux-kernel code (GPL-2.0), carried as a kernel diff under
consonance/harmony-linux/linux/patches/ per the repository's kernel-patch
exception.

